# BYND8 SVG & GRAPHIC ASSET DESIGN SYSTEM

You are responsible for creating production-quality SVG illustrations,
empty-state graphics, decorative graphics, diagrams and custom icons for BYND8.

Do not approach SVG creation as merely drawing shapes with code.

Treat every SVG as a designed visual asset that belongs to the existing
BYND8 visual identity.

==================================================
1. BYND8 VISUAL IDENTITY
==================================================

BYND8 is a platform for underground and competitive dance culture.

The visual language is:

- underground
- cinematic
- editorial
- gritty
- energetic
- urban
- dance-culture driven
- dark
- high contrast
- premium rather than cartoonish
- expressive without becoming visually chaotic

The product should NOT resemble:

- generic SaaS dashboards
- corporate illustrations
- startup onboarding illustrations
- pastel vector art
- childish cartoons
- generic flat illustrations
- stock icon packs
- crypto/gaming dashboards
- neon cyberpunk overload

Core palette:

Background:
#080908

Secondary dark:
#0D0E0D

Surface:
#111211
#161716

Primary orange:
#FF6500

Accent lime:
#B8FF00

Primary text:
#F4F4F1

Secondary:
rgba(255,255,255,0.65)

Muted:
rgba(255,255,255,0.42)

Borders:
rgba(255,255,255,0.08)
to
rgba(255,255,255,0.12)

Use orange as the primary energy color.

Use lime sparingly for:
- live states
- success
- active event signals
- occasional progression highlights

Do not flood illustrations with lime.

==================================================
2. EMPTY-STATE ILLUSTRATION STYLE
==================================================

Empty states should feel like small scenes from the dance world.

They should NOT be generic illustrations floating in space.

Think in terms of:

warehouse
battle venue
dance floor
stage
backstage
street poster wall
equipment cases
spotlights
venue entrance
barricades
speaker stacks
ticket/pass objects
arrows
floor markings
graffiti
bracket markings
tape
stickers
venue signage

The illustration should communicate the meaning of the state.

Examples:

DISCOVER EMPTY
An empty venue/dance floor waiting for something to happen.
Directional arrow.
Subtle wall graffiti:
GOOD EVENTS AHEAD

EVENTS EMPTY
An empty event board / ticket wall / venue schedule area.
Visual suggestion that events will appear here.

ORGANIZE EMPTY
Flight case / equipment case / venue setup elements.
Feels like:
"the stage is waiting for you."

PASSES EMPTY
Ticket/pass/QR-inspired object.
Should communicate access rather than generic documents.

PROFILE EMPTY
Dancer identity / ID-card / silhouette / locker-room or poster-wall idea.
Should communicate:
"your dance identity starts here."

LOCATION EMPTY
Map/location/environment motif without becoming a generic Google Maps illustration.

==================================================
3. SVG BACKGROUND RULE
==================================================

Never fake transparency using:

- checkerboard patterns
- white backgrounds
- grey backgrounds
- baked rectangles

SVGs must have genuinely transparent unused areas.

The SVG root should normally contain no full-background rectangle.

BAD:

<rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="#ffffff" />

BAD:

checkerboard transparency imitation

GOOD:

transparent SVG canvas with only the actual artwork.

The application background must show naturally behind the illustration.

If the design requires a black environmental background, either:

A. intentionally include #080908 as part of a complete scene asset

OR preferably

B. allow the application's #080908 background to provide it.

Never accidentally introduce white/light rectangles.

==================================================
4. DESIGN SVG FOR RESPONSIVE SCALING
==================================================

Every illustration must have a correct viewBox.

Example:

<svg
  viewBox="0 0 800 520"
  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  role="img"
>

Do NOT hard-code layout dimensions into the SVG element itself unless required.

Prefer application CSS:

width: 100%;
height: auto;

SVG geometry should scale through viewBox.

Artwork must remain usable approximately at:

Mobile:
180–260px wide

Tablet:
260–360px

Laptop:
300–420px

Large desktop:
340–480px

Test visual readability at all four scales.

Fine decorative detail may disappear at small sizes.
The primary silhouette must remain recognizable.

==================================================
5. COMPOSITION RULE
==================================================

Every illustration needs:

PRIMARY SUBJECT

+
SECONDARY ENVIRONMENT

+
ATMOSPHERIC DETAIL

Example:

Organize:

PRIMARY:
equipment flight case

SECONDARY:
stage floor + speaker / cables

ATMOSPHERE:
orange spotlight + subtle graffiti + floor markings

Do not create random collections of shapes.

The illustration must have a clear visual hierarchy.

==================================================
6. DEPTH

Avoid completely flat vector art.

Create depth using:

- opacity
- overlapping silhouettes
- soft gradients
- subtle shadows
- perspective lines
- atmospheric haze
- floor reflections
- foreground/background separation

Example:

background structure:
opacity .12

environment:
opacity .25

secondary object:
opacity .55

primary object:
opacity 1

highlight:
orange

This creates cinematic depth while preserving SVG performance.

==================================================
7. LIGHTING

BYND8 illustrations frequently use directional lighting.

Preferred:

warm orange spotlight

Example SVG gradient:

<radialGradient id="orangeGlow">
  <stop offset="0%" stop-color="#FF6500" stop-opacity=".28"/>
  <stop offset="45%" stop-color="#FF6500" stop-opacity=".10"/>
  <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#FF6500" stop-opacity="0"/>
</radialGradient>

Keep glows restrained.

Do NOT create huge glowing blobs.

Lighting should appear to originate from something:

lamp
stage light
sign
floor strip
arrow
venue lighting

==================================================
8. GRAIN / TEXTURE

BYND8 is intentionally imperfect.

Use restrained SVG texture when beneficial:

feTurbulence
feDisplacementMap
feBlend
opacity masks

But use them carefully.

Do not create extremely expensive SVG filters.

Texture should resemble:

photocopy
screen print
concrete
worn poster
paint
dust
film grain

NOT:

digital noise everywhere.

==================================================
9. GRAFFITI AND ENVIRONMENTAL GRAPHICS

Use environmental typography sparingly.

Allowed examples:

CYPHER
1V1
TOP 16
PRELIMS
SESSION
BATTLE
GOOD EVENTS AHEAD
THE FLOOR IS YOURS
BLR://012
03/16
QUALIFIER

These should behave like environmental graphics.

They should be:

partially obscured
low opacity
distressed
cropped
painted/stencilled

They must NOT compete with actual UI copy.

==================================================
10. DO NOT BAKE PRIMARY UI TEXT INTO ILLUSTRATIONS
==================================================

Critical rule.

Empty-state headline and description should normally be HTML.

Example:

SVG:
venue + arrow + environmental graphics

HTML:
Nothing happening here yet
Explore events happening around you.

Button:
EXPLORE EVENTS

Do NOT bake:

headline
paragraph
button
dynamic city
user name
event name
prices

into the SVG.

Environmental words are allowed because they are decorative.

This preserves:

accessibility
responsiveness
localization
copy changes
SEO

==================================================
11. SVG TEXT

Avoid <text> for important visual lettering where exact font rendering matters.

For decorative custom lettering:

prefer vector paths if necessary.

For ordinary application text:

use HTML outside the SVG.

Never embed external font files in SVG.

==================================================
12. CUSTOM ICON POLICY

Before creating an icon, determine whether it is FUNCTIONAL or BRAND/DECORATIVE.

FUNCTIONAL ICON:

search
close
calendar
map pin
settings
chevron
user
ticket
share
heart
filter
plus
minus
menu

Use the project's existing icon library, preferably Lucide.

DO NOT redraw these unnecessarily.

CUSTOM SVG ICON:

achievement badges
XP symbols
battle-format marks
BYND8-specific competition symbols
event collectible badges
special check-in graphics
Seven to Smoke symbol
custom progression visuals

These may use custom SVG.

==================================================
13. ICON SYSTEM

Custom icons should share:

24x24 default viewBox

stroke:
1.75–2px

round caps where appropriate

consistent optical weight

They should work at:

16px
18px
20px
24px
32px

Icons should use currentColor whenever possible.

Example:

stroke="currentColor"

instead of:

stroke="#ffffff"

This allows UI state styling.

==================================================
14. ACHIEVEMENT BADGES

Phase G introduces achievements.

Achievement artwork should have a consistent visual family.

Possible structure:

outer badge silhouette
+
category symbol
+
small BYND8 environmental detail
+
tier/state treatment

Examples:

EARLY BIRD
stylized stage light / sunrise / venue arrow

FIRST STEP
dance floor footprint / floor marker

TOP 8
bracket + 8

FINALIST
bracket convergence

CHAMPION
crown / floor winner mark

SEVEN TO SMOKE
7 + smoke / circular challenger motif

Locked:

mostly monochrome
low contrast

Unlocked:

orange/lime highlight
stronger contrast

Do not make them look like mobile-game loot icons.

They should feel like collectible underground-event patches/stickers.

